VulkanLumen — Stage 4: surface material cards and M1 direct-light fast path

All GPU rendering remains in vulkan-systems/lumen/. The host test program,
vulkan-experiment/main.cpp, requires no rendering changes.

Scene and API
  - Same original Cornell room, rotated blocks, emissive ceiling panel.
  - Same public create/record/compose/result/resize/invalidate/reset API.
  - Same ray-tracing data: 30 triangles / 59 BVH nodes, with shared VulkanBVH
    node and triangle layouts; CPU-built once, traversed in fragment shaders.
  - No neural or analytic spatiotemporal denoising.

Stage 4 material-card cache
  - One RGBA16F 256x256 atlas with 16 material cards (4x4 grid).
  - Each 64x64 card owns a stable surface face ID and local UV coordinates.
  - A tiny compute shader writes material albedo to all cards, once at startup
    and again when the cache is invalidated. The atlas remains shader-readable
    across frames; image transitions synchronize compute writes and raster
    reads in the same command buffer.
  - Primary raster fragments read material color from the corresponding card,
    not the interpolated mesh vertex albedo. The face SSBO provides card UVs.
  - This is the *material capture foundation*. Surface lighting/cached
    radiance, automatically placed cards for arbitrary meshes, and offscreen
    material changes are NOT implemented. Those are later Lumen stages.
  - set_scene() still accepts handles but this demo uses its internal geometry.

Performance changes based on observed M1 measurements (27–45 FPS)
  - Existing scene GPU cost was 18–28 ms; composite was only about 0.02 ms.
  - Stage 3 sampled four shadow rays per visible fragment. Stage 4 tests
    visibility once toward the center of the emitter, then integrates the
    four unshadowed emitter directions for smooth diffuse falloff. This is
    a cheaper, harder-edged shadow approximation, not physically exact
    penumbra sampling. The generic BVH ray-query code is kept for later GI.
  - Rejects backfacing/unlit pixels before the expensive BVH query.
  - Internal color+depth raster size is capped at 1280 pixels wide, with the
    host swapchain still at full display resolution. This matters especially
    on Retina framebuffers where a 1280x720 window can be 2560x1440 pixels.
  - settings.tracing_scale (default 1.0) permits a smaller internal render
    size; scale cannot exceed 1.0 and the 1280-wide cap still applies.
  - This is spatial resolution reduction + bilinear presentation, NOT a
    denoiser. Compare clarity and performance at the actual display size.
  - CPU frame and per-pass GPU timings are still printed by the experiment.

Test
  cd vulkan-experiment
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Look for:
  [lumen] Stage 4: 30 triangles / 59 BVH nodes | cards=16 x 64x64 |
          scene render=1280x720 (swapchain 2560x1440) | ...
  [lumen] XX FPS | ... | GPU scene=X ms composite=X ms total=X ms ...

Exact sizes depend on the actual screen scale. The goal is GPU scene below
16.67 ms plus sufficient headroom for future GI; no 60 FPS is promised
without native M1 benchmarking. The GPU scene interval currently also
includes initial atlas construction when the cache is first built.

Next stage (after testing): surface lighting capture and world-space radiance
cache for diffuse bounce lighting. This material atlas by itself does not
create Lumen indirect illumination or color bleeding.
